Call a function an executable defines directly on x86-64, not through a PLT.

GNU as since binutils 2.31 writes R_X86_64_PLT32 for every `call` to a global
function, PIC or not, where it wrote R_X86_64_PC32 before. TinyCC 0.9.27
gives each PLT32 relocation a PLT slot and a GOT entry, and fills them only
for a dynamic link: in a static executable the slot's `jmp *disp(%rip)` keeps
the GOT entry's offset as its displacement, and the GOT stays zero. musl's
crt1.o calls __libc_start_main that way, so every program this TinyCC linked
for x86-64 Rach2 jumped into the bytes after the PLT and faulted before main.

The call needs no slot when the executable itself defines the function: it is
a plain PC-relative call, which is what this change makes of it, as TinyCC
already did for a local or hidden symbol and as its later releases do for any
symbol an executable defines. An undefined symbol keeps its slot, and a static
link then refuses it as before. i386 is untouched: its calls are R_386_PC32
unless written `@PLT`, and the code is under TCC_TARGET_X86_64.

--- a/tccelf.c
+++ b/tccelf.c
@@ -1081,7 +1081,9 @@
 #ifdef TCC_TARGET_X86_64
             if ((type == R_X86_64_PLT32 || type == R_X86_64_PC32) &&
                 (ELFW(ST_VISIBILITY)(sym->st_other) != STV_DEFAULT ||
-		 ELFW(ST_BIND)(sym->st_info) == STB_LOCAL)) {
+		 ELFW(ST_BIND)(sym->st_info) == STB_LOCAL ||
+		 (s1->output_type == TCC_OUTPUT_EXE &&
+		  sym->st_shndx != SHN_UNDEF))) {
                 rel->r_info = ELFW(R_INFO)(sym_index, R_X86_64_PC32);
                 continue;
             }
